THIRD-PARTY NOTICES

The Erebine agent images that the compose files in this repository run
(ghcr.io/erebine/container-agents) contain third-party software, each
component under its own license.

  - The Erebine binary in each image, erebine-eim in the EIM images and
    erebine-eem in the EEM image, statically links third-party Swift
    packages. Their notices are installed in the image at
    /usr/share/licenses/erebine/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES, attached to each
    release of the binaries as the asset THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES
    (https://github.com/Erebine/binaries/releases), and printed by
    `erebine-eim --license` and `erebine-eem --license`.
  - The base images, distribution packages and Python packages in each
    image carry their own license files inside the image.
  - vLLM's Rust frontend, vllm-rs, in every vLLM image except
    eim-vllm-rocm, statically links Rust crates and, through them, the
    OpenSSL, zstd, PCRE2, Oniguruma and mimalloc libraries. Each crate's
    license text, and where its source is published, is in the image at
    /usr/share/licenses/erebine/vllm-rs/NOTICES.
  - The Intel GPU images contain oneCCL and vllm-xpu-kernels built from
    source that Erebine changed so that it builds with the open-source
    DPC++ compiler. Each image carries each patch with a notice that the
    component was modified: oneCCL's beside its license text in
    /opt/oneccl/share/licenses/, and vllm-xpu-kernels' in
    /usr/share/licenses/erebine/intel/oss/vllm-xpu-kernels/. PATCHES.md
    lists the changes.
  - The GPU EIM images contain NVIDIA, AMD or Intel software under
    vendor terms, some of which restrict use and not only redistribution.
    VENDOR_NOTICES names those terms, links them, and says where each
    license text sits inside the image.

What Erebine changes in the inference stack is recorded in PATCHES.md.
